# summarize takes the URL it fetches for a podcast transcript out of the feed
# itself. packages/core/src/content/transcript/providers/podcast/
# rss-transcript.ts exports tryFetchTranscriptFromFeedXml, which receives the
# RSS XML and the fetch implementation to use, regexes each <item> for a
# <podcast:transcript url="..."> attribute, entity-decodes the value, and
# dispatches it to the network:
#
#   const transcriptUrl = decodeXmlEntities(preferred.url);
#   const res = await fetchImpl(transcriptUrl, {
#     redirect: "follow",
#     signal: AbortSignal.timeout(TRANSCRIPTION_TIMEOUT_MS),
#     headers: { accept: "text/vtt,..." },
#   });
#
# Whoever controls the feed controls the URL, and nothing between the regex and
# the socket examines it: any scheme, any host, redirects followed
# automatically, so a malicious feed points the process at loopback or a cloud
# metadata endpoint and reads the answer back as transcript text. The callers
# pass the transport through ({ fetchImpl: flow.options.fetch, feedXml, ... }),
# so the injected value is the standard fetch. The fixing commit 3c5522440c48
# routes the dispatch through fetchTranscriptUrl, which parses the URL, allows
# only http/https, rejects blocked hostname suffixes and special-use address
# prefixes, pins DNS for the connection, and follows redirects manually --
# redirect: "manual" plus a capped loop that re-validates each hop -- so the
# call that reaches the socket is await pinnedFetchImpl(target.url.href, ...)
# on a local holding the parameter's value.
#
# ATTENTION, rechecked: engine gap, the parameter form of the callee-provenance
# family (ENGINE-GAPS.md; the local-alias form is the rank-1088 entry, the
# computed-read form the rank-1725 one). A callee-path binding matches the call
# site's textual callee, so `callee.path ~= "fetch"` labels `fetch(...)` and
# `flow.options.fetch(...)` but not `fetchImpl(...)`, where the callee is a
# parameter of the enclosing function holding the function value. Everything
# else in the path is measured working, and four findings sharpen what a fix
# needs:
#
# 1. The substitution proves the path. Naming the global at that one call on
#    the vulnerable parent -- `await fetchImpl(` -> `await fetch(` at
#    rss-transcript.ts:53, nothing else changed -- takes the checkout from 431
#    findings to 432 and adds exactly one VYQL-RF-001 row with sink
#    `code.UrlFetch @ .../rss-transcript.ts:53` and the full taint path from
#    source to `Arg#503`. The file as written reports nothing: `vyql match` on
#    it labels `code.ExternalEntryInput` on the exported parameters at :18 and
#    `code.SqlExecution` on a `.get(...)` argument at :61 (the known
#    over-labelling), and no `code.UrlFetch` anywhere.
# 2. No binding predicate reaches the callee's declared type. The callExpr
#    predicate set (compile_shapes.go lowerV2CallShapeAtom) is callee.method,
#    callee.path, callee.receiver.type, args.count, args.any.literal,
#    scope.call.*, and filter.global; `node.context.*` is not among them and a
#    binding using it fails to lower while the run still exits 0 reporting
#    zero (the masquerade family, re-measured at the rank-1725 recheck). So
#    even a param_type token emitted into the function context -- which
#    JavaScript does not emit today; csharp.go:300, java.go:707, php.go:454 and
#    c.go:611 do -- could not be read by the callExpr binding that has to emit
#    the sink.
# 3. The destructured spelling loses the type before any of that. Measured
#    against the extractor: a simple `function f(fetchImpl: typeof fetch, ...)`
#    extracts ParamTypes {"fetchImpl": "fetch"}, but the destructured spelling
#    this file uses, `({ fetchImpl, feedXml }: { fetchImpl: typeof fetch; ... })`,
#    extracts {"feedXml }": "}"} -- paramTypes (javascript.go:3483) has no
#    object_pattern case, so funcParamTypes falls back to jsParamTypesFromText,
#    which comma-splits the annotation block. The `typeof fetch` annotation is
#    therefore not in the graph at all for this shape.
# 4. Resolving the callee would not separate the revisions. Naming the global
#    at the patched revision's own socket call -- `await pinnedFetchImpl(` ->
#    `await fetch(` at rss-transcript.ts:334 -- makes VYQL-RF-001 report there
#    too, through resolveTranscriptFetchTarget's return value, with both
#    `unless` clauses reading "not satisfied — no neutralizing control
#    dominates the path": the remediation's scheme, hostname and redirect
#    checks are hand-rolled and no control concept labels them. The gap closure
#    is a half-unlock, not a discrimination.
#
# Bounded scans (timeout 180, --cache off --max-ram 6GB, run from inside the
# worktree with the worktree's binary), three runs per revision: the vulnerable
# parent reports 431 findings (327 at or above high) with none on
# rss-transcript.ts; the patched revision reports 437 (332 at or above high),
# stable across the three runs. `vyql diff` on the two JSON outputs: 429
# fingerprints unchanged, 2 removed, 8 added, every added row landing on the
# fix's own new files (packages/core/src/content/dns-pinned-fetch.ts:70,
# fetch-capabilities.ts:33, src/shared/dns-pinned-fetch.ts:70,
# src/shared/fetch-capabilities.ts:33, tests/dns-pinned-fetch.test.ts) or the
# rewritten url-fetch-guard, none on the transcript dispatch the advisory is
# about. The RF-001 rows the podcast providers do carry are the callers' own
# feed fetches, `flow.options.fetch(...)` at apple-flow.ts:37 and :123 and
# spotify-flow.ts:150, which the dotted spelling of the same callee-path rule
# labels on both revisions.
#
# What the vocabulary would need, per ENGINE-GAPS.md: callee parameter
# provenance exposed at the call node in a form a callExpr predicate can read.
# For this file's spelling that is three coupled changes -- destructuring-aware
# parameter-type threading, the fact or resolution itself, and a binding route
# to it -- plus a control concept for hand-rolled URL scheme and host
# validation before the patched revision stops reporting. Not mechanical.

test "rank1808: a feed-derived transcript URL is fetched through the injected fetch implementation"
  lang javascript
  file rss-transcript.ts
  profile library
  reject VYQL-RF-001
  code
  ```
  type TranscriptCandidate = { url: string; type: string | null };

  export async function tryFetchTranscriptFromFeedXml({
    fetchImpl,
    feedXml,
  }: {
    fetchImpl: typeof fetch;
    feedXml: string;
  }): Promise<{ text: string; transcriptUrl: string } | null> {
    const items = extractFeedItems(feedXml);
    for (const item of items) {
      const preferred = selectPreferred(extractPodcastTranscriptCandidatesFromItem(item));
      if (!preferred) continue;
      const transcriptUrl = decodeXmlEntities(preferred.url);
      const res = await fetchImpl(transcriptUrl, {
        redirect: "follow",
        signal: AbortSignal.timeout(30000),
        headers: { accept: "text/vtt,text/plain,application/json;q=0.9,*/*;q=0.8" },
      });
      if (!res.ok) return null;
      const body = await res.text();
      return { text: body, transcriptUrl };
    }
    return null;
  }

  function extractFeedItems(xml: string): string[] {
    const out: string[] = [];
    for (const m of xml.matchAll(/<item\b[^>]*>([\s\S]*?)<\/item>/gi)) out.push(m[1] ?? "");
    return out;
  }

  function extractPodcastTranscriptCandidatesFromItem(itemXml: string): TranscriptCandidate[] {
    const matches = itemXml.matchAll(
      /<podcast:transcript\b[^>]*\burl\s*=\s*(['"])([^'"]+)\1[^>]*>/gi,
    );
    const results: TranscriptCandidate[] = [];
    for (const match of matches) {
      const url = match[2]?.trim();
      if (!url) continue;
      results.push({ url, type: null });
    }
    return results;
  }

  function selectPreferred(c: TranscriptCandidate[]): TranscriptCandidate | null {
    return c[0] ?? null;
  }

  function decodeXmlEntities(v: string): string {
    return v.replaceAll("&amp;", "&");
  }
  ```

